        "title": "CrashLoopBackOff",
        "source": "crashloopbackoff.md",
        "section": "Fixes",
        "text": "## Fixes\n\n- If the command is wrong (for example a demo container that ends in `exit 1`), correct `command`/`args` or the image entrypoint so the main process stays in the foreground.\n- If a config value is missing, add the env var or volume and roll the workload.\n- If a dependency is down, fix that service or make startup retry instead of exiting.\n- If a liveness probe is too aggressive, increase `initialDelaySeconds` / `failureThreshold` (see probe-failures).\n- After a fix, roll the deployment and confirm the restart count stops climbing:\n\n  ```bash\n  kubectl rollout status deploy/<name> -n <ns>\n  kubectl get pod <pod> -n <ns> --watch\n  ```",
        "score": 0.7808643194408089,
        "kind": "runbook"
      },
      {
        "title": "ImagePullBackOff and ErrImagePull",
        "source": "imagepullbackoff.md",
        "section": "Symptoms",
        "text": "## Symptoms\n\n- Pod stays `Pending` or shows `STATUS` as `ImagePullBackOff` / `ErrImagePull`.\n- Events include `Failed` from `kubelet` with messages such as `Failed to pull image`, `not found`, `unauthorized`, `denied`, or `invalid image name`.\n- There are no application logs because the container has not started.",
        "score": 0.7669850389755728,
        "kind": "runbook"
      },
      {
        "title": "CreateContainerError",
        "source": "create-container-error.md",
        "section": "Symptoms",
        "text": "## Symptoms\n\n- Pod is `Waiting` with reason `CreateContainerError`.\n- Events include `Failed` from the kubelet with a runtime error (device mapper, `no space left on device`, seccomp / AppArmor / SELinux denial, invalid capability, or a missing device plugin).\n- The image has often already been pulled (`ImagePullBackOff` is not the current reason).\n- Application logs are typically unavailable because the container never started.",
        "score": 0.7525147283662867,
        "kind": "runbook"
      },
      {
        "title": "CrashLoopBackOff",
        "source": "crashloopbackoff.md",
        "section": "Diagnosis",
        "text": "## Diagnosis\n\n1. Read the last termination reason and exit code:\n\n   ```bash\n   kubectl describe pod <pod> -n <ns>\n   kubectl get pod <pod> -n <ns> -o jsonpath='{range .status.containerStatuses[*]}{.name}{\" \"}{.state}{\" \"}{.lastState.terminated}{\"\\n\"}{end}'\n   ```\n\n2. Always fetch previous logs if `restartCount > 0`:\n\n   ```bash\n   kubectl logs <pod> -n <ns> --previous --tail=200\n   kubectl logs <pod> -n <ns> --tail=200\n   ```\n\n3. Confirm the command that actually ran (`command` / `args` in the pod spec) and whether the image entrypoint expects different arguments.\n4. If logs mention a missing file, env var, or connection refused, inspect the related ConfigMap, Secret, and Service.",
        "score": 0.7446062061614203,
        "kind": "runbook"
      },
      {
        "title": "Pending and unschedulable pods",
        "source": "pending-unschedulable.md",
        "section": "Symptoms",
        "text": "## Symptoms\n\n- `kubectl get pods` shows `STATUS` as `Pending` for longer than a few seconds.\n- `kubectl describe pod` has a `PodScheduled` condition set to `False` with reason `Unschedulable`.\n- Events include `FailedScheduling` with a message such as `0/N nodes are available: ...`.\n- The pod has no `nodeName` yet (unless it is stuck later in volume attach or image pull).",
        "score": 0.7401378231627089,
